==English==

===Etymology===
From {{affix|en|lymph|-atic}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/lɪmˈfæ.tɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ætɪk|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} Pertaining to [[lymph]] or the [[lymphatic system]].
# Lacking [[energy]] or [[enthusiasm]]; having characteristics once associated with an [[excess]] of lymph: lack of [[muscle tone]], [[paleness]], [[sluggish]]ness, etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|author={{w|René-Joseph-Hyacinthe Bertin|R. J. Bertin}}|translator=Charles W. Chauncy|title=Treatise on the Diseases of the Heart, and Great Vessels|location=Philadelphia|publisher=Carey, Lea & Blnachard|page={{gbooks|FlpAAQAAMAAJ|188|lymphatic}}|passage=Eleonore Lemindre, aged 34, tailoress, of a sanguine '''lymphatic''' temperament, having suffered great depression of spirits, experienced, in the course of 1820, symptoms of what is called disease of the heart.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Haunted House|page=5|chapter=The Mortals in the House|passage=Streaker, the housemaid, too, had an attribute of a most discomfiting nature. I am unable to say whether she was of an unusually '''lymphatic''' temperament, or what else was the matter with her, but this young woman became a mere Distillery for the production of the largest and most transparent tears I ever met with.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=w:Thomas Hardy|chapter=Lines|title=Wessex Poems and Other Verses|location=London|publisher=Macmillan & Co.|year_published=1919|page=224|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3167/3167-h/3167-h.htm
|passage=Who has not marked, where the full cheek should be,<br>Incipient lines of lank flaccidity,<br>'''Lymphatic''' pallor where the pink should glow,<br>And where the throb of transport, pulses low?—}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1934|title=w:Burmese Days|author=w:George Orwell|passage=He was a '''lymphatic''', half-witted Hindu youth, who lived his life in almost complete silence{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1961|author=w:V. S. Naipaul|title=w:A House for Mr Biswas|publisher=Vintage International|year_published=2001|section=Part One, Chapter 4
|passage=Hari, the holy man, who was to be the pundit that day, was just as Mr Biswas remembered him, just as soft-spoken and '''lymphatic'''. His felt hat sat softly on his head. He greeted Mr Biswas without rancour, without pleasure, without interest.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Madly [[enthusiastic]]; [[frantic]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1638|author=[[w:Sir Thomas Herbert, 1st Baronet|Thomas Herbert]]|title=Some Yeares Travels into Africa and Asia the Great|section=Book I, p. 28|url=https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_hNn7DbdAKVAC
|passage=A ''Negro'' stood by all the while trembling, now and then lifting up his hands and eyes, muttering his black Art to some hobgoblin, and (when we least suspected it) skips out, and in a '''limphatic''' rapture drew a long knife which he brandisht about his head 7 or 8 times, and after so many spells put it up againe; he then kisses the humid earth 3 times and rises merrily: upon a sudden, the skie cleared and no more noise affrighted us.}}

